DEFENSIVE TAKEAWAY — public shortener stats as an agent-detection surface:
  DETECTION SURFACE: Any YOURLS instance that leaves `+`/infos stats pages public is an operator-side fingerprint of whatever automation touches its links. YOURLS logs full referrer URLs by default, including query strings — so proxied exfil chains (jqp -> allorigins -> sec.gov/county.json with jq filters) appear verbatim. The UNM instance alone yielded 1,159 per-URL referrer rows; ETH's single slug yielded 28 more with a direct jqp->da.gd->SEC chain.
  EARLY-WARNING SIGNAL: burst-onset detection — both venues peaked on 2026-06-18 (UNM 1,845 hits, ETH 63 hits). A defender running public YOURLS stats (or their own link shortener with public analytics) can alert on (a) a referrer host never seen before (e.g. jqp.vercel.app, md.succ.ai appearing for the first time), (b) referrer URLs containing proxy-wrapper + jq/JSON-extraction grammar, (c) synchronized hit spikes across unrelated slugs/instances on the same day.
  WHAT A DEFENDER COULD INSTRUMENT: (1) flip YOURLS `private stats` on for sensitive instances (UNM's was public; ETH's was public; UVM's correctly hides referrers from anonymous viewers — that is the model config); (2) if stats stay public, run a nightly job diffing referrer hosts against an allowlist and flagging first-seen proxy-wrapper domains; (3) treat `+`-page hit bursts on old, low-traffic slugs (ETH's nB1nv was created 2020-08-24, 0.12/day, then 63 hits in one day) as an anomaly worth one human look; (4) preserve the last-30d window — it ages out daily, so snapshot it on any alert, because the decimated all-time series will never give you July 5-6 back.
